      "text" : "We have six top tricks: two each in spades, hearts and clubs. Diamonds must supply three more; establish them before cashing the side-suit entries. Lead D3 toward dummy's king. If the king wins, lead a low diamond from dummy and duck in hand, retaining Q97 and the club ace as an entry; after regaining control, use the remaining diamond toward dummy to establish and run the suit. This produces ample tricks on a 3-3 break and handles some favorable 4-2 layouts. If the king loses to the ace, regain the lead and lead the remaining diamond toward dummy, normally playing the queen; use the observed spots to reassess any marked finesse or adverse break. Preserve CA to reach established diamonds rather than cashing it prematurely. Keep SQ as protection against the opening suit, and use heart honors or CK to regain the lead when possible. Once three diamond tricks are available, cash nine winners without seeking risky overtricks. Unfavorable diamond breaks may require an additional concession or a club finesse toward the ten; reassess against the defenders' established threats before choosing that fallback.",
